  "live_records": {
    "q-96344": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96344",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{6}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{0}^{1/6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2835\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
