    "q-96345": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96345",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ calculate $P(X<1).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{3}{4}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.5276\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
