    "q-96406": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96406",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(1),$ calculate $P(X\\ge 2).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(1).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\ge 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
