    "q-96346": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96346",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ calculate $P(1<X<4).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1<X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.4712\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
