    "q-96417": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96417",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(1\\le X\\le 3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le X\\le 3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1329\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
