    "q-253929": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253929",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X>6\\,|\\,X>4).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a random variable $X∼Exp(\\lambda ),$ and two positive real numbers $s,t,$ the momeryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2,$ $s=4,$ and $s+t=6.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=(t+s)-s=6-4=2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6\\,|\\,X>4) & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(2)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
