    "q-253938": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253938",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X\\le 9\\,|\\,X>6).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a random variable $X∼Exp(\\lambda ),$ and two positive real numbers $s,t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2,$ $s=6,$ and $s+t=9.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=(t+s)-s=9-6=3\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9\\,|\\,X>6) & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-253938-a",
